# converted from 04-the-fire.md
@title The Fire
@bg hollow
@mood dread

narrate The village gave him nine days.
narrate He counted them afterwards, because counting was the only thing he had left that worked. Nine days between the funeral and the fire. Long enough for the story to travel. Long enough for a man to walk to the checkpoint and back.
narrate It was the well.
narrate That was the detail that broke it. Not the hole in the field, not the mark on his palm — nobody in the Hollow had seen his palm. It was the well, and it went like this:
narrate Nine days after Ilsa died, the Wellers' youngest got the Fever.
narrate And a man in the Hollow — Cael never learned which one, and in the end he stopped wanting to — said the thing that everybody had been not-saying:
narrate And that was all it took. Not an accusation. Not evidence. Just the two words that everybody in the Mire knew the shape of, applied to a man whose daughter had died and whose house was on the edge of the Hollow and who had been strange since the funeral.
narrate Because if he had gone north, then the Fever in the Weller child was his, and the village was not unlucky, it was invaded, and there was a person to be angry at, and there is no comfort in the world like a person to be angry at.
narrate They came at night.
narrate He heard them. That was the mercy and the cruelty of it — he had been sitting up, as he had every night since the funeral, in the chair with the terrible cushion, in a house that smelled of the herb bed and the cold fire, and he heard boots in the garden and he knew exactly what it was.
narrate He could have gone out the back.
narrate He had eleven seconds and he used all of them, and he used them to carry the cushion out of the house.
narrate That was the whole of his thinking. Not the ledger, not the medicine, not the money under the floor. He went into the bedroom and took the cushion off the chair and held it against his chest and walked out of the back door into the reeds, and behind him the front of his house went up with a sound like a door being kicked in.
narrate He watched it burn from the reeds.
narrate And here is the thing that he would spend twelve years failing to explain, to Maeve, to Bryn, to a boy in a burned kitchen: he was not angry.
narrate He had been ready to be angry. He had spent nine days being ready. He had rehearsed it — the things he would say, the way he would stand, the dignity of it.
narrate And what he actually felt, standing in the cold water with his daughter's cushion against his chest, watching everything he owned turn to light, was relief.
narrate Because now it was done. Because now there was nothing left that could be taken. Because a man with a house has something to lose and a man with nothing has nothing to lose, and he had been carrying the weight of that house for eleven days the way he had carried his daughter for eleven miles, and now it was off him, and he could breathe.
narrate He stood in the reeds and watched his life burn and he felt light.
narrate That was the moment. If anyone ever asked him — and nobody ever did — that was the moment Cael stopped being a healer.
narrate They came into the garden afterwards.
narrate He could see them from the reeds, four of them, standing where his gate had been, checking the work the way men check work.
narrate And he knew one of them.
narrate He knew him by his walk, because he had watched that walk come up to a rope between two posts twelve days earlier with a ledger in his hand.
narrate Bryn.
narrate He stood in the reeds and watched the sergeant look at the burning house.
narrate And the sergeant did something that Cael would carry for twelve years, and that he would never once manage to make into a story that made sense:
narrate Bryn stood in the garden of a man he had turned away at a gate, watching that man's house burn, and he put his hand over his mouth.
narrate Just for a moment. Just long enough to be seen by somebody standing in the reeds in the dark with a cushion in his arms.
narrate And then he took his hand away and turned around and gave an order, and the four of them walked away.
narrate Cael stayed in the reeds until morning.
narrate At dawn he went back. There was nothing to go back to, and he went back anyway, because there was a thing he had to know.
narrate He found what he was looking for in the ruin of the bedroom. The fire had taken the roof and most of the walls, but the floor by the bed was still there, and under the loose board was a small wooden box, and in the box was a wooden horse and a cushion and a ledger.
narrate He sat down in the ashes of his own house and opened the ledger.
narrate He read it four times.
narrate Then he put the ledger in his coat, and the horse in his pocket, and the cushion under his arm, and he stood up in the ruin of the only life he had ever had, and he looked north.
narrate He did not go north that day.
narrate He went north four years later, and by then he was a different man, and the thing he asked for was not three days.
narrate But that is Chapter 6, and this is Chapter 4, and in Chapter 4 a man stands in the ashes of his house with a child's cushion under his arm and makes a decision that will kill forty thousand people.
narrate The decision was this:
narrate He said it out loud, in the ruin, to nobody.
narrate And he walked out of the Hollow and into the Mire, and he did not look back, and that was the last day of the man who had been a healer.
